ng that the  virus is not falling  well that's a little easier to  understand if you look at how the  imperial study takes its data  it does two big samples the last one was  in january the six it started then  but it compares it to the previous  sample which was taken at the end of  november  and the beginning of december so the  imperial study missed  both the um december surge and the  january peak  now the imperial react study is one of  many studies now looking at covid there  are  for example we've got the zoe study run  by king's college  they save a number of new covered  infections has been cut in half  since the new year peak that's one piece  of good news but overall  there's not much good news we can still  see the levels of covert even now  they're lower than they were in the new  year but they're still  far higher several times higher than  they were a few months ago  and we've got the death figures which  always lag infections  they're terrible and every day we're  seeing depths climb towards  almost the peak which we had during the  first wave there is no  reason to look in these figures and see  any reason to lower your guard  but nor is there any reason to think  that things are getting exponentially  worse  in january as had been rationally feared  at the beginning of the year  things do seem to be improving and there  does seem to be rational grounds for  hoping  that when lockdown restrictions are  reviewed in the middle of february  we might be able to move back to the  tier system  [Music]  you

